diff --git a/docs/api/sections/webhooks.md b/docs/api/sections/webhooks.md
index a4f48b5..1b2cc10 100644
--- a/docs/api/sections/webhooks.md
+++ b/docs/api/sections/webhooks.md
@@ -159,3 +159,35 @@ __Response:__
 ```
 
 `request` and `response` can be `null` for deliveries that have not started yet or are still in progress. The delivery request headers omit the `X-Webhook-Signature` header.
+
+## Receiving events
+
+Active webhooks receive a JSON `POST` for each subscribed event on their board.
+The payload contains the event's `id`, `action`, `created_at`, `eventable`, `board`,
+and `creator`. Links in the payload include the account slug.
+
+Requests include these headers:
+
+- `Content-Type: application/json`
+- `X-Webhook-Delivery`: a stable ID for this webhook/event delivery.
+- `X-Webhook-Timestamp`: the event's UTC creation time.
+- `X-Webhook-Signature`: the hexadecimal HMAC-SHA256 of the exact request body,
+  using the webhook's `signing_secret` as the key.
+
+Each delivery is attempted once, without retries or redirect following. Duplicate
+jobs cannot send it again. HTTP 2xx responses are successful; other status codes
+and connection errors are failures, recorded in the delivery log. Destinations
+must resolve to a public IP address.
+
+As with any HTTP integration, an interrupted connection cannot establish whether
+the receiver processed the request. An interrupted worker can leave a delivery
+`in_progress`; it is not resent. Receivers should use `X-Webhook-Delivery` as an
+idempotency key when processing events. Exactly-once processing across network
+failures requires cooperation from the receiver, not just the sender.
+
+A webhook is automatically deactivated after at least ten consecutive failures
+spanning at least one hour. A successful delivery resets the failure streak, as
+does manually reactivating the webhook. Queued deliveries do not send to inactive
+webhooks.
+
+Delivery logs are retained for seven days and cleaned up every fifteen minutes.
